= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $x$ run along the wire and $y$ across it. In a separable approximation,
$$
\boxed{\psi_{nk}(x,y)=\frac{e^{ikx}}{\sqrt{L_x}}\phi_n(y),\qquad E_n(k)=E_n^\perp+\frac{\hbar^2k^2}{2m^*}.}
$$
Transverse confinement gives discrete levels $E_n^\perp$, but the long unconfined direction has a nearly continuous longitudinal <wave vector> and a **plane-wave factor**. The result is a family of one-dimensional subbands, not a completely discrete spectrum in both in-plane directions. For a hard-wall width $W$, $E_n^\perp=\hbar^2\pi^2n^2/(2m^*W^2)$; an electrostatic confinement potential gives different thresholds. The suppressed out-of-plane degree of freedom is already frozen into the 2DEG subband.

\Image[paper-2-wire-subbands.png]
{title=Discrete transverse thresholds coexist with continuous longitudinal dispersion}
{source=paper-2-wire-subbands.py}

= ii
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

One suitable platform is a monolayer **<molybdenum disulfide>** <semiconductor>. An electrostatic accumulation gate supplies conduction electrons whose motion is confined to the atomic sheet; source and drain contacts act as reservoirs. A clean insulating substrate and dielectric or encapsulation reduce disorder and prevent gate leakage. Two split gates deplete the adjacent sheet and leave a narrow conducting gap, thereby confining the in-plane transverse direction while retaining transport along the gap.

\Image[paper-2-split-gate-device.png]
{title=Split gates narrow a gated two-dimensional semiconductor sheet between source and drain reservoirs}
{source=paper-2-split-gate-device.py}

Apply a small source-drain <voltage>, measure the current and determine linear-response conductance while tuning the split-gate <voltage> and carrier <density>. A <graphene> sheet is another two-dimensional carrier system, but its lack of an ordinary gap complicates purely electrostatic confinement; etched boundaries or a suitable gapped architecture may be needed. A two-dimensional material does not by itself guarantee ballistic transport or resolved subbands.<Split-gated point-contact in MoS2-hBN structures by Chithra H. Sharma and Madhu Thalakulam>[Electrostatic confinement in a two-dimensional <semiconductor> platform]{ref}

= iii
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For a clean, adiabatically connected GaAs channel at low <temperature> and small bias, opening successive spin-degenerate subbands gives
$$
\boxed{G=N\frac{2e^2}{h}.}
$$
Conductance therefore shows a **staircase of plateaux**, rather than the continuously geometry-dependent classical Ohmic result. Each fully transmitted mode contributes approximately $77.5\,\mu\mathrm S$. More positive, or less negative, depletion-gate <voltage> opens a conventional GaAs constriction; more negative <voltage> closes it.

\Image[paper-2-conductance.png]
{title=Conductance rises in quantum steps as a depletion-gate constriction opens}
{source=paper-2-conductance.py}

Require subband spacings larger than thermal and bias broadening and a <mean free path> exceeding the constriction length. The general <Landauer formula> is $G=(e^2/h)\sum_\alpha T_\alpha$ with each spin/valley channel counted separately. A material with additional valley degeneracy need not have the same step sequence as GaAs, and imperfect transmission or contact resistance blurs the ideal plateaux.

