= Solution
{parent=i}

In a **<vibrating-sample magnetometer>**, a magnetized specimen vibrates sinusoidally in a nominally uniform applied field. Its motion changes the magnetic flux through stationary pickup coils, producing an induced <voltage> by <Faraday's law of induction>. The component at the vibration frequency is proportional to the sample <magnetic moment> times vibration amplitude and frequency, for a calibrated coil geometry.

A lock-in detector referenced to the vibration extracts this small signal. Calibrate with a known moment, stabilize the vibration amplitude and sweep the applied field to measure a <magnetization> loop. Dividing moment by film volume gives $M$ in $\mathrm{A\,m^{-1}}$; the silicon substrate and holder background must be subtracted for such a thin film.<7300 Series VSM System User's Manual by Lake Shore Cryotronics>[Section 4.1: pickup signal, vibration amplitude/frequency and synchronous detection]{ref}

= ii
{parent=16}
{scope}

= a
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Along the **easy axis**, expect an approximately rectangular loop: after saturation the remanent <magnetization> is close to $\pm M_s$, and reversal occurs near $\pm H_c$ through <nucleation> and domain-wall motion. Permalloy is magnetically soft, so $H_c$ is ordinarily small, although defects and growth conditions set its actual value.

\Image[paper-2-permalloy-loops.png]
{title=Easy-axis switching and hard-axis rotation for an idealized uniaxial thin film}
{source=paper-2-permalloy-loops.py}

The axes are $M$ in $\mathrm{A\,m^{-1}}$ and applied $\mu_0H$ in $\mathrm{mT}$. $M_s,H_c,H_k$ are symbolic because their measured values are not supplied. The $50\,\mathrm{mT}$ deposition field establishes an axis; it is not the measured coercive or anisotropy field.

= b
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Along the **in-plane hard axis**, the ideal uniform-magnetization model gives reversible rotation from the easy direction toward the applied field. With uniaxial anisotropy <energy> <density> $K_u\sin^2\theta$, minimization gives
$$
\boxed{M_{\parallel H}=M_s\frac{H}{H_k}\quad(|H|\leq H_k),\qquad H_k=\frac{2K_u}{\mu_0M_s},}
$$
followed by saturation at $\pm M_s$. The right panel of the preceding figure therefore has a nearly linear central section, essentially zero remanence and ideally no <hysteresis>. Actual domains, misalignment and defects can produce rounding or a small loop. This is an in-plane hard axis; the much larger out-of-plane <demagnetizing field> is a different effect.

