= Solution
{parent=i}

Two examples are **<poly(3-hexylthiophene)>, <P3HT>**, a conjugated polymer commonly used as an electron donor, and **<PCBM>**, a soluble fullerene derivative commonly used as an electron acceptor. Both are <organic semiconductors>; “organic” does not imply that their transport properties are identical. Their blend is a familiar donor-acceptor photovoltaic model system.

= ii
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

An **<exciton>** is a Coulomb-correlated electron-hole pair, electrically neutral overall. Three useful properties are:

* **<Exciton binding energy>:** the <energy> required to separate the correlated pair into unbound carriers. A larger value makes thermal dissociation less effective; <dielectric screening> and electronic delocalization influence it.
* **Spatial extent:** the characteristic electron-hole separation or <exciton> radius. A localized Frenkel-like <exciton> is small compared with a <Wannier-Mott exciton> spread over many <unit cells>.
* **Lifetime:** the characteristic time before radiative or nonradiative decay. Together with its <diffusion coefficient> it determines a transport scale, often expressed as $L_D=\sqrt{D_X\tau_X}$ in a specified diffusion-length convention.

In many molecular <organic semiconductors>, limited screening and localization make <exciton> binding important, so absorption need not immediately produce collectable free charges.

= iii
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A suitable donor-acceptor heterojunction provides an energetically favourable route from a bound excitation to an interfacial charge-transfer state and then separated carriers. The electron is preferentially transported in the acceptor and the hole in the donor. The interface facilitates dissociation that is inefficient in a single strongly excitonic material; it must also avoid excessive <energy> loss and rapid interfacial <recombination>.

\Image[paper-2-organic-junction.png]
{title=An interpenetrating donor-acceptor structure balances short exciton travel with continuous charge-extraction paths}
{source=paper-2-organic-junction.py}

An idealized <bulk heterojunction> places most generated <excitons> within roughly a <diffusion> length of an interface while maintaining continuous donor paths to the hole contact and acceptor paths to the electron contact. Domain dimensions should therefore be comparable to the material's <exciton> transport scale, not arbitrarily large. The total absorbing thickness should capture substantial light yet remain within the charge-collection capability set by mobility, field and <recombination>. Excessive mixing can impair transport or increase <recombination>; very coarse domains waste <excitons> before they reach an interface.<Tailored exciton diffusion in organic photovoltaic cells by S. Matthew Menke and others>[<Exciton> <diffusion> as a constraint on planar and <bulk heterojunction> dimensions]{ref}

= iv
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Relative to conventional crystalline-silicon technology, many organic absorbers offer low-temperature thin-film processing and lightweight flexible devices, but have more strongly bound <excitons> and more disorder-limited charge transport. These are common material trends, not universal statements about every organic or inorganic device.

Relevant <semiconductor> properties include the absorption spectrum and optical gap, donor/acceptor <energy> levels and offsets, <dielectric screening> and <exciton> dissociation, electron/hole mobilities, <recombination> lifetimes, traps and morphological stability. Contacts must extract the appropriate carrier selectively. These properties determine the device quantities in
$$
\boxed{\eta=\frac{J_{\rm sc}V_{\rm oc}\,\mathrm{FF}}{P_{\rm in}}.}
$$
A favourable gap alone does not fix efficiency: current generation, <voltage> loss and fill factor must all be controlled.

