= Solution
{parent=i}

In floating-catalyst CNT growth, a controlled <sulfur> additive acts as a **growth promoter** for the <catalyst>, commonly iron. <Sulfur> modifies <catalyst> surface composition, carbon uptake and the strength of the catalyst-carbon interaction; it can facilitate cap formation/lift-off and sustained tube growth instead of complete <catalyst> encapsulation. It also changes catalyst-particle evolution and hence tube yield, diameter and wall-count distributions.

There is an optimum range: excessive <sulfur> can occupy active sites or form an insufficiently active sulfide-rich <catalyst> and reduce growth. The additive is not simply a constituent that must be incorporated into every carbon wall. Its detailed action depends on <catalyst>, carbon source and process conditions; a single universal melting-point explanation is insufficient.<The promoter role of sulfur in carbon nanotube growth by Balázs Orbán and Tibor Höltzl>[First-principles evidence for <sulfur> weakening iron-carbon binding; later mechanistic support, not an official answer]{ref}

= ii
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The circumference is the length of the <graphene> wrapping vector:
$$
C=a\sqrt{n^2+nm+m^2},\qquad D=\frac C\pi.
$$
For $(12,12)$,
$$
\boxed{D=\frac{0.246\sqrt{432}}\pi=1.63\,\mathrm{nm}.}
$$
Approximating the closest wall-to-wall separation by the graphite interlayer spacing $d_{002}=c/2=0.3355\,\mathrm{nm}$ gives the nearest-neighbour axis separation in a triangularly packed bundle:
$$
\boxed{s=D+d_{002}\simeq1.96\,\mathrm{nm}.}
$$
This assumes undeformed circular tubes with a graphitic contact gap. Faceting, flattening, adsorbates or pressure can alter a real bundle's spacing.

= iii
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Each concentric shell has a different circumference, so it generally needs a different integer wrapping vector $(n,m)$. Different vector lengths do not <force> different chiral angles, but exact atomic registry between all curved shells is geometrically restrictive. The shells can nucleate with different wrapping orientations, and strong covalent bonding within each shell dominates the weaker intershell interaction.

Consequently, it is usually unnecessary to strain every shell into a common orientation and registry; many multiwall tubes have incommensurate shells with different chiral angles. **Identical chiral angles are possible**, for example among suitable members of one armchair family, and should not be ruled out purely because the radii differ. Atomic indices, chiral angle and interwall commensurability are distinct descriptions.

= iv
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The real-space carbon honeycomb has two atoms in a primitive cell of its triangular <Bravais lattice>. The <reciprocal lattice> is also triangular, and its Wigner-Seitz cell is a hexagonal first <Brillouin zone>. With a consistent orientation convention, **the reciprocal-zone hexagon is rotated by $30^\circ$ relative to a real-space carbon-ring hexagon**; reciprocal lengths scale inversely with the direct lattice spacing.

\Image[paper-2-nanotube-reciprocal.png]
{title=Real carbon rings, the rotated graphene reciprocal zone and circumferential momentum quantization}
{source=paper-2-nanotube-reciprocal.py}

Rolling into a tube imposes $\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf C_h=2\pi\ell$, leaving parallel allowed lines in <graphene>'s two-dimensional reciprocal space. They run along the tube-axis direction, perpendicular to the circumferential wrapping vector. The nanotube's actual one-dimensional <Brillouin zone> is an interval of axial <wave vectors> with period $2\pi/|\mathbf T|$, where $\mathbf T$ is its axial translation. The parent hexagon is therefore a zone-folding construction, not a literal two-dimensional <Brillouin zone> for the finished one-dimensional tube.

= v
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

For a $(10,0)$ tube, $C=10a=2.46\,\mathrm{nm}$. A cut normal to its axis crosses ten axial load-bearing C-C bonds in the ideal unstrained honeycomb geometry. Assuming equal load sharing and simultaneous failure at $8\,\mathrm{nN}$ per bond,
$$
F_{\max}\simeq10(8\,\mathrm{nN})=80\,\mathrm{nN}.
$$
The <graphene> areal <mass> is $\sigma_m=0.75\,\mathrm{mg\,m^{-2}}=7.5\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-2}}$, giving linear <mass> <density>
$$
\lambda=\sigma_mC=1.845\times10^{-15}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-1}}=1.845\times10^{-9}\,\mathrm{tex},
$$
since the <tex unit> has $1\,\mathrm{tex}=10^{-6}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-1}}$. Thus the <ideal carbon nanotube tenacity> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{F_{\max}}{\lambda}=43.4\,\mathrm{N/tex}.}
$$
This neglects vacancies, curvature-induced changes in bond strength, thermal activation, imperfect load sharing and structural relaxation at failure. An aligned macroscopic fibre is a network of finite tubes or bundles: sliding, weak intertube shear transfer, imperfect alignment, defects and unequal load participation limit its strength. The nominal $10\,\mu\mathrm m$ fibre diameter alone does not turn it into one defect-free covalent cylinder, explaining why a value near $2\,\mathrm{N/tex}$ can be much lower than this ideal single-tube estimate.

= 21
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= i
{parent=21}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take $0<E<V_0$ and equal electron <mass> in all regions. Define $k=\sqrt{2mE}/\hbar$ and $\kappa=\sqrt{2m(V_0-E)}/\hbar$. Then
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm I:&\quad-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\psi''=E\psi,\qquad\psi_I=Ae^{ikx}+Be^{-ikx},\\
\mathrm {II}:&\quad-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\psi''+V_0\psi=E\psi,\qquad\psi_{II}=Ce^{\kappa x}+De^{-\kappa x},\\
\mathrm {III}:&\quad-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\psi''=E\psi,\qquad\psi_{III}=Fe^{ikx}.
\end{aligned}
$$
There is no wave incident from the far right. Region I contains oscillatory incident and reflected waves; region II is **evanescent**, with real exponentials rather than propagating <plane waves>. Both exponential terms are allowed in a finite barrier; deleting the growing term before boundary matching is generally incorrect. A complex superposition in the barrier can carry a nonzero conserved tunnelling current.

= ii
{parent=21}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a finite potential step and constant <mass>, $\psi$ and $\psi'$ are continuous. A finite jump of $\psi'$ would require a delta-function contribution in the integrated equation, while a jump of $\psi$ would be still more singular. Matching the preceding amplitudes gives
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
A+B&=C+D,\\
ik(A-B)&=\kappa(C-D),\\
Ce^{\kappa a}+De^{-\kappa a}&=Fe^{ika},\\
\kappa(Ce^{\kappa a}-De^{-\kappa a})&=ikFe^{ika}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
The <transmission probability> is $|F/A|^2$ because the incident and transmitted regions have equal <wave vector> and <mass>; in unequal leads a velocity ratio is also required. These matching conditions do not apply unmodified to infinite walls, delta barriers or <mass> discontinuities.

= iii
{parent=21}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Between two barriers, region III becomes a **finite intermediate well** containing both right- and left-moving oscillatory components, $Pe^{ikx}+Qe^{-ikx}$. Repeated reflection produces standing-wave-like enhancement and quasi-bound levels with finite escape lifetimes. At resonant energies, the multiple transmitted amplitudes add constructively: an ideal symmetric lossless pair can have transmission approaching unity even when a single barrier transmits poorly. Away from resonance the pair strongly suppresses transmission.

\Image[paper-2-resonant-tunnelling.png]
{title=Calculated single- and double-barrier transmission and a separate qualitative resonant-tunnelling current-voltage characteristic}
{source=paper-2-resonant-tunnelling.py}

A useful phase condition is $2k\ell+\varphi_{r1}+\varphi_{r2}=2\pi n$, where $\ell$ is the intermediate-well width and the reflection phases account for penetration into the barriers. The printed figure starts the second barrier at $a+L$, so its well width is $L$; if the prose's second-barrier position $L$ is used instead, the width is $L-a$. The resonance argument is unchanged once that geometrical convention is fixed.

= iv
{parent=21}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The intended **double-barrier resonant-tunnelling diode** has a bias-dependent well level. Initially increasing bias can bring a quasi-bound level into alignment with available occupied emitter states, sharply increasing current. At larger bias the resonant level moves out of the effective injection window, for example below the emitter conduction-band edge, so resonant transmission and current decrease despite increasing <voltage>. This produces a peak-to-valley interval with **$dI/dV<0$**. Further bias can increase nonresonant or higher-level current again.

The right panel of the preceding figure is a qualitative example. The device can supply small-signal negative differential conductance to an appropriate oscillator circuit while its current and <voltage> remain positive. A bias-independent transmission function alone does not guarantee NDR: the voltage-dependent potential profile, contacts and carrier supply must be included. The source's reference to part (ii) appears to point to the earlier barrier matching; the resonant device mechanism uses the added barrier of part (iii).

= 22
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= i
{parent=22}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

In generic **<resistive-pulse sensing>**, two <electrolyte> reservoirs are connected through a small aperture. Electrodes apply a bias and a low-noise current amplifier records the pore current. Passage of a weakly charged insulating particle excludes conducting liquid, usually increasing resistance and giving a transient current decrease at fixed <voltage>. Pulse amplitude, duration and event rate contain size-, transport- and concentration-dependent information, subject to calibration.

Advantages include label-free single-particle counting, electrical readout and small active volume. Limitations include clogging, electrode/pore noise, finite bandwidth, uncertain particle trajectories and surface-conduction effects; a pulse is not intrinsically chemically specific. Strong particle charge or concentration polarization can complicate, or even reverse, the simple blockade signal. The schematic below uses only generic nonbiological particle transport.

\Image[paper-2-resistive-pulse.png]
{title=Generic particle sensing through an electrolyte-filled aperture with voltage bias and current readout}
{source=paper-2-resistive-pulse.py}

= ii
{parent=22}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Ignoring access effects and wall charge, the cylindrical conducting liquid has
$$
\boxed{R=\frac{\ell}{\sigma\pi r^2}=\frac{100\times10^{-9}}{1.25\pi(5\times10^{-9})^2}=1.02\times10^9\,\Omega.}
$$
The corresponding conductance is approximately $0.982\,\mathrm{nS}$. A charged surface can contribute mobile <counterions> and change this estimate. Surface conduction is important when its conductance is comparable with the bulk contribution: for surface conductance $K_s$, their ratio in a cylinder is $2K_s/(\sigma r)$, twice the <Dukhin number> convention $K_s/(\sigma r)$. Double-layer overlap, when the <Debye length> is comparable with the pore radius, is another warning that uniform bulk conductivity is inadequate. No surface-charge or ionic-strength data are supplied to quantify those corrections.

= iii
{parent=22}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

With the left reservoir at $V$ and the right at zero, let the pore run from $x=0$ to $x=\ell$. In the stated idealization the reservoir potentials are flat, the pore potential is $\phi(x)=V(1-x/\ell)$, and its electric field is **$E_x=-d\phi/dx=V/\ell$**, directed from high to low potential. The field is zero in the ideal equipotential reservoirs; real access regions would round these abrupt boundaries.

\Image[paper-2-pore-signals.png]
{title=Ideal potential, field and current blockade for a generic insulating particle crossing a uniform pore}
{source=paper-2-pore-signals.py}

For constant-speed passage and an approximately position-independent in-pore obstruction, the current has a rectangular downward pulse from $I_0$ to $I_0-\Delta I$ during occupation. Entry, exit, filtering and nonuniform fields round real pulses. This describes a physical particle signal without inferring any biological identity or charge.

