= Solution
{parent=i}

**<Self-assembly>** is organization of interacting building blocks into a larger structure without individually placing every component. Static <self-assembly> approaches an equilibrium free-energy minimum under fixed conditions; a resulting structure may subsequently become kinetically trapped. Dynamic <self-assembly> maintains a nonequilibrium organized state through continuing <energy> or matter throughput and dissipation, and can change or disappear when that drive is removed.

For nanofabrication, parallel assembly of many small components can generate fine periodicity, hierarchical structures and adaptive or self-repairing arrangements with limited direct patterning. Building-block chemistry encodes interactions. However, access to a desired morphology depends on kinetics and competing states; assembly does not automatically eliminate defects or provide arbitrary device geometry.

= ii
{parent=23A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Relevant interactions include **van der Waals attraction**, **electrostatic attraction or screened repulsion**, **<hydrogen bonding>**, and **hydrophobic/solvophobic association**. Coordination bonding, magnetic dipole interactions and capillary <forces> can also organize suitable building blocks. Entropic effects, including depletion attraction and packing <entropy>, can drive ordering even without a new chemical bond. Which contribution favours assembly depends on solvent, <temperature>, concentration and surface chemistry; the complete balance is a free-energy change, not a requirement that every interaction be attractive.

= iii
{parent=23A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Consider a <colloidal photonic crystal> assembled from nearly monodisperse dielectric spheres. Spherical geometry and approximately isotropic interactions favour close-packed arrays, although fcc, hcp and stacking-disordered structures can compete. Sphere diameter $D$ sets the periodic optical length scale. For an ideal close-packed fcc array,
$$
d_{111}=\sqrt{\frac23}D,\qquad\lambda_{\rm Bragg}\simeq2n_{\rm eff}d_{111}
$$
at normal incidence in the simple effective-index picture. Thus changing the building-block size tunes the reflected wavelength; refractive-index contrast affects stop-band strength. Surface charge and screening control colloidal stability and rearrangement during assembly, while polydispersity, roughness and stacking defects broaden the optical response and increase scattering.

\Image[paper-2-photonic-assembly.png]
{title=Building-block size and refractive index set the optical response of a colloidal array}
{source=paper-2-photonic-assembly.py}

This connects sphere-level geometry and material response to collective periodic optical behaviour. The Bragg reflection estimate does not establish a complete three-dimensional photonic band gap.

= 23B
{parent=Section B}
{scope}
{c}

= i
{parent=23B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**<Brownian motion>** is the fluctuating motion of a suspended particle produced by thermal molecular collisions. Using the standard <Brownian displacement conventions>, in a uniform fluid without external drift, mean displacement is zero but mean-square displacement grows with time. For overdamped <diffusion>, $\langle\Delta x^2\rangle=2Dt$ in one coordinate and $\langle|\Delta\mathbf r|^2\rangle=6Dt$ in three dimensions, with $D=k_BT/\zeta$ and Stokes friction $\zeta=6\pi\eta a$ for a no-slip sphere.

Brownian transport brings <nanoparticles> into encounters and allows them to explore configurations during <self-assembly>. Reversible interactions permit rearrangement toward low-free-energy structures; interactions that are too strong can instead trap disordered aggregates. <Thermal fluctuations> also oppose localization in shallow traps. The symbol $\zeta$ avoids confusing friction with an unrelated chemical potential.

= ii
{parent=23B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For radius $a=10\,\mathrm{nm}$ and gold <density> $2.0\times10^4\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, $m=8.38\times10^{-20}\,\mathrm{kg}$. The supplied Stokes model gives $\zeta=3.96\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm{kg\,s^{-1}}$ and $D=1.05\times10^{-9}\,\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$ at $300\,\mathrm K$. Therefore the standard one-second diffusive rms distances are **$45.7\,\mu\mathrm m$ in one coordinate** and **$79.2\,\mu\mathrm m$ in three dimensions**.

Using the source's supplied shorthand literally with $m\langle v^2\rangle=3k_BT$ instead gives $\sqrt{3k_BTt/\zeta}=56.0\,\mu\mathrm m$. That shorthand lacks the factor two in the standard long-time mean-square displacement; it should not be identified with either of the preceding standard rms conventions. None of these rms values is a cumulative path length.

An ideal drag-free fall from rest gives **$gt^2/2=4.91\,\mathrm m$**. Actual gravitational settling in the assumed viscous model is much smaller: $\tau_p=m/\zeta=2.12\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm s$, $u_t=mg/\zeta=0.208\,\mu\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, and displacement in one second is approximately **$0.208\,\mu\mathrm m$**. Thus <diffusion> overwhelms settling, even though ideal vacuum fall exceeds the rms <diffusion> distance. For <rarefied-gas nanoparticle drag>, at a 20-nm size in argon, continuum no-slip drag may require a rarefaction/slip correction; pressure and gas mean-free-path data are absent, so these numerical transport estimates are conditional on the supplied Stokes model.

= iii
{parent=23B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For fluid <density> $\rho_f$, speed $U$ and characteristic length $d$, inertial kinetic <energy> scales as $\rho_fd^3U^2$ while viscous work over distance $d$ scales as $\eta Ud^2$. Their ratio is the **<Reynolds number>**,
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{Re}=\frac{\rho_fUd}{\eta}.}
$$
Smaller particles at a fixed speed have smaller Re, so viscous stresses dominate fluid inertia when $\mathrm{Re}\ll1$. Increasing speed increases Re and eventually makes inertia more important, rather than making viscous dominance stronger.

Particle inertia is a separate issue. Its <particle velocity-relaxation time> is $\tau_p=\rho_pd^2/(18\eta)$; comparing it with a travel time $d/U$ gives a <Stokes number> $\tau_pU/d$. This uses particle <density> $\rho_p$, whereas the fluid <Reynolds number> uses fluid <density> $\rho_f$.

= iv
{parent=23B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Interpreting the nominal size as diameter $d=10\,\mathrm{nm}$, fluid viscous dominance requires
$$
\boxed{U\ll\frac{\eta}{\rho_f d}=\frac{6.0\times10^4}{\rho_f}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}},}
$$
with $\rho_f$ in $\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$. **The liquid <density> is not supplied**, so the conventional fluid-Reynolds crossover is not numerically fixed. As an explicitly additional illustrative assumption, $\rho_f=870\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ gives $\mathrm{Re}=1$ near $69\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$; ordinary much slower <nanoparticle> motion is well within the viscous regime.

If instead the supplied gold <density> is inserted into the dimensionless ratio $\rho_pUd/\eta$, that ratio reaches one at **$3.0\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$**. It is not the fluid <Reynolds number>. Including the spherical Stokes prefactor, the particle relaxation criterion $\tau_pU/d=1$ occurs at $54\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. These quantify different inertia comparisons. The wording's speed direction is reversed: viscous dominance is obtained by making speed sufficiently **small**. If 10 nm denotes radius, the length-based thresholds halve.

= v
{parent=23B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

A tightly focused laser produces a strong intensity gradient. A small polarizable particle experiences a gradient <force> toward increasing intensity when the real <polarizability> has the appropriate positive sign; <radiation pressure> from scattering and absorption pushes predominantly along the beam. A high-numerical-aperture objective creates lateral confinement and a sufficiently strong opposing axial gradient for a three-dimensional <optical trap>. Equilibrium is generally shifted from the intensity maximum by <radiation pressure>.

\Image[paper-2-optical-trap.png]
{title=Gradient forces restore a displaced particle while radiation pressure shifts the axial equilibrium}
{source=paper-2-optical-trap.py}

For a Rayleigh sphere, $\alpha=4\pi\epsilon_m a^3(\epsilon_p-\epsilon_m)/(\epsilon_p+2\epsilon_m)$ in an absolute-permittivity convention, and $\mathbf F_{\rm grad}\simeq\tfrac14\operatorname{Re}\alpha\nabla|E|^2$ for peak complex field amplitude. Near a stable point, $F_x\simeq-k_xx$; the trap depth must exceed <thermal fluctuations> sufficiently to retain the particle. Viscous drag opposes motion, Brownian forcing produces fluctuations, and gravity/buoyancy are normally small at this scale.

For **absorbing gold**, use its complex wavelength-dependent permittivity: scattering, absorption and heating can frustrate trapping. The existence of an ideal dielectric gradient-force trap does not guarantee stable trapping of every metal <nanoparticle> at every wavelength or power.<Observation of a single-beam gradient force optical trap for dielectric particles by Arthur Ashkin and others>[General <optical trapping> principle; gold-specific absorption qualification is separate]{ref}
